# Coding Standards

Universal rule. Keep stack-specific guidance in dedicated rules: `ruby-conventions.mdc`, `vue-frontend.mdc`, `design-principles.mdc`.

## Structure & Naming

- Prefer simple modules and small functions over clever abstractions
- Keep files focused on one responsibility — split large controllers, services, or components before they become hard to scan
- Use descriptive domain names: `ConversationFinder`, `assignConversation`, `Messages::AudioTranscriptionService`
- Follow project naming patterns consistently (PascalCase components, camelCase events, Ruby snake_case)
- Replace business-significant literals with named constants

## Comments & Cleanup

- Comments explain non-obvious intent or trade-offs, not what the code already says
- Write comments in English
- Do not leave commented-out code, empty `rescue`/`catch` blocks, or unused exports behind
- Remove dead/unreachable code

## Documentation

- Never generate standalone documentation files (README, changelogs, wiki pages) unless explicitly requested
- Inline schema registration, YARD/RDoc on public APIs, and code-first docs living with the code are allowed

## Errors & Abstractions

- Handle failures explicitly — use custom exceptions (`lib/custom_exceptions/`), do not swallow errors
- Avoid premature abstractions; duplicate once if needed, then extract when the pattern is real
- Optimize for readability first: names, module boundaries, and control flow should help a reviewer understand the code quickly
